Nasal Polyps
Opthalmology & ENT
Smooth pale nasal masses, Chronic nasal obstruction, Anosmia/hyposmia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-026
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe facial pressure and panic due to absolute bilateral nasal obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a patient with acute, severe panic secondary to a sensation of complete upper airway obstruction, accompanied by intense, expansive facial fullness. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to rule out acute anaphlaxis, secure airway patency, and manage the immediate physiological distress preventing sleep prior to the next morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My nose is completely cemented shut, my face feels like a balloon ready to pop, and I haven't been able to smell a thing for months."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced gradually over the past year as mild congestion, progressively worsening into chronic, bilateral nasal obstruction. Over the past 48 hours, the blockage became absolute, forcing obligate mouth breathing that has severely disrupted sleep. Patient reports profound anosmia due to mechanical blockage and a chronic, persistent post-nasal drip. Endorses a newly intensified dull, continuous facial pressure over the central face, precipitating acute anxiety tonight. Denies acute facial pain, purulent discharge, or fevers.
Functional Status: Highly distressed and fatigued due to forced mouth breathing and sleep deprivation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute anxiety exacerbating the sensation of dyspnea.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Anxious, obligate mouth breather, speaking with a prominent hyponasal voice.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals large, bilateral, smooth, pale, glistening, teardrop-shaped benign mucosal masses completely occluding both nasal cavities. Probing of these visible nasal masses elicits no pain or tactile sensation, confirming their polypoid nature. Oropharynx is dry but clear. Facial palpation reveals mild pressure tenderness over the maxillary sinuses but no acute pain. Chest auscultation is clear; no wheeze (rules out concurrent asthma exacerbation).
Environmental Observations: Patient sitting upright surrounded by tissues and empty saline spray bottles.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent use of over-the-counter antihistamines with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Massive Bilateral Nasal Polyposis - Status: Acutely decompensated (obstructive panic)
Differentials:

Deviated Nasal Septum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The bilateral nature of the obstruction and direct visualization of mobile, pale polypoid masses rather than a fixed cartilaginous deformity.

Acute Bacterial Sinusitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of thick purulent discharge, lack of fever, and the chronic, progressive timeline of the absolute obstruction.

Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The bilateral, smooth, pale, and insensate nature of the lesions, contrasting with the unilateral, friable, and bleeding masses typical of malignancy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe distress and panic driven by an absolute mechanical upper airway obstruction. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to visually inspect the airway, rule out acute allergic angioedema or severe bacterial infection, and definitively diagnose the etiology of the blockage. Identifying massive, insensate nasal polyps provided immediate diagnostic clarity. Delaying assessment would have subjected the patient to continued panic and severe sleep deprivation. Immediate administration of a high-dose oral corticosteroid was initiated to rapidly shrink the polypoid tissue and restore partial nasal patency, safely resolving the acute crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Prednisolone 50mg daily for 5 days to acutely reduce polyp volume. Prescribed Intranasal Mometasone 2 sprays per nostril daily for long-term maintenance. Generated referral to ENT for consideration of functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS).
Positive Behavioral Change: To maximize the efficacy of the topical steroid, the patient must perform a large-volume saline sinus rinse immediately prior to spraying. By physically clearing the stagnant mucus first, the patient will immediately feel the cool spray hit the nasal lining (positive reinforcement), ensuring the medication physically reaches the polyp tissue rather than being wasted on mucous blockages.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to assess steroid response and confirm ENT referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops acute shortness of breath, wheezing, sudden facial swelling, or severe unilateral facial pain with fever.

